WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.440
AI writes code faster than a person can check what it decided.

2
00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:07.440
So before it builds something hard to undo, the decision goes to a room.

3
00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:11.100
This is AvoQ, the coding workbench I am building.

4
00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:13.480
Its security gate needs a secret scanner.

5
00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:17.170
I decided: Betterleaks two, from its version two point oh release.

6
00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.380
The decision is logged, and a room is queued.

7
00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:24.760
The question goes in blind: the facts and the options, never my choice.

8
00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:27.790
This is the real room, held in Finnish.

9
00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:35.990
Replayed from its record: my machine seats two models from two families, Claude and GPT.

10
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:37.750
Neither can see my decision.

11
00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:40.620
Each seals a first vote before it reads the other's.

12
00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:43.250
Then two rounds of arguments.

13
00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.850
They have the release page fetched: version two point oh does not exist yet, only a release candidate.

14
00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:58.540
Between the rounds Jev, a fast judging model from TypeSafe, scores the arguments, not the votes.

15
00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:05.120
The room disagrees with me.

16
00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:12.130
Betterleaks two, yes, but on a condition: pin the release candidate and its rule file, and let the gate fail closed.

17
00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:13.370
And it is unsure.

18
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.090
The weaker seat gives it only zero point one five.

19
00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:20.790
I read the arguments, and I took the condition.

20
00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.120
It became row ninety-eight of AvoQ's decisions.

21
00:01:25.223 --> 00:01:28.143
The gate was built the next day, pinned exactly as the room asked.

22
00:01:28.513 --> 00:01:31.573
The room had changed the decision before a single line of the gate existed.

23
00:01:32.923 --> 00:01:37.153
Rooms have now checked a hundred and forty-seven of my decisions and disagreed with thirty.

24
00:01:37.523 --> 00:01:42.263
Every outcome is recorded, so over time the system learns when to trust a room, and when to trust me.

25
00:01:43.463 --> 00:01:46.893
The room added a condition my decision did not have, in two minutes.

26
00:01:47.313 --> 00:01:52.923
That is hybrid decision-making: two language models argue, a fast judging model scores, and I decide.

27
00:01:53.363 --> 00:01:56.503
Without it, my decisions are slower and less complete.

